      "proofOfConcept": "### Proof of Concept\n\nReplace the body of `test_submissionValidity()` in `test/c4/C4Submission.t.sol` with the following (no other changes to `setUp()` or helpers required):\n\n```solidity\nfunction test_submissionValidity() public {\n    // ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n    //  M: sUSDM.decimals() returns 6 instead of ERC-4626\n    //     compliant 12, causing 10^6 miscalculation for\n    //     any integrating protocol.\n    // ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════\n\n    // --- Step 1: Deposit USDC -> USDM -> sUSDM ---\n    uint256 depositAmount = 100e6; // 100 USDM (6-decimal)\n    _deposit(user1, depositAmount);\n    _stake(user1, depositAmount);\n\n    // --- Step 2: Observe the raw share balance ---\n    uint256 rawShares = susdm.balanceOf(user1);\n\n    // With _decimalsOffset()=6, OZ ERC4626 mints:\n    //   shares = assets * (totalSupply + 10^offset) / (totalAssets + 1)\n    //         = 100e6 * 1e6 / 1  (first deposit, virtual shares dominate)\n    //         = 100e12\n    assertEq(rawShares, 100e12, \"raw shares = 100e12 for 100 USDM deposit\");\n\n    // --- Step 3: Show the decimals() mismatch ---\n    uint8 reportedDecimals = susdm.decimals();\n    assertEq(reportedDecimals, 6, \"sUSDM reports decimals=6\");\n\n    // Per OZ ERC4626 design: decimals = underlyingDecimals + _decimalsOffset\n    // USDM.decimals() = 6, _decimalsOffset() = 6 -> expected 12\n    uint8 expectedDecimals = usdm.decimals() + 6; // 6 + 6 = 12\n    assertEq(expectedDecimals, 12, \"ERC-4626 compliant decimals should be 12\");\n    assertTrue(reportedDecimals != expectedDecimals, \"BUG: decimals mismatch\");\n\n    // --- Step 4: Demonstrate the 10^6 integration error ---\n    // Any integrating protocol normalizes token amounts as:\n    //   humanReadable = rawBalance / 10^decimals\n    //\n    // With the WRONG decimals (6):\n    uint256 perceivedTokens = rawShares / (10 ** reportedDecimals);\n    // = 100e12 / 1e6 = 100_000_000 (100 million sUSDM!)\n    assertEq(perceivedTokens, 100_000_000, \"integrator sees 100M tokens\");\n\n    // With the CORRECT decimals (12):\n    uint256 correctTokens = rawShares / (10 ** expectedDecimals);\n    // = 100e12 / 1e12 = 100 (100 sUSDM, matching 100 USDM deposited)\n    assertEq(correctTokens, 100, \"correct interpretation: 100 tokens\");\n\n    // The miscalculation factor is exactly 10^6\n    assertEq(perceivedTokens / correctTokens, 1_000_000, \"10^6 overestimation\");\n\n    // --- Step 5: Show convertToAssets is correct (internal math fine) ---\n    uint256 assetsBack = susdm.convertToAssets(rawShares);\n    // convertToAssets uses _decimalsOffset() directly, not decimals()\n    assertApproxEqAbs(assetsBack, depositAmount, 1, \"convertToAssets correct\");\n\n    // --- Step 6: Concrete lending protocol scenario ---\n    // A lending protocol values sUSDM collateral as:\n    //   collateralValue = balanceOf(user) / 10^decimals() * pricePerToken\n    //\n    // With price = $1 per sUSDM token (1:1 with USDM at launch):\n    uint256 pricePerToken = 1e6; // $1 in 6-decimal USD\n    uint256 wrongCollateralValue = perceivedTokens * pricePerToken;\n    //   = 100_000_000 * 1e6 = 100_000_000e6 ($100M!)\n    uint256 correctCollateralValue = correctTokens * pricePerToken;\n    //   = 100 * 1e6 = 100e6 ($100)\n    assertEq(\n        wrongCollateralValue / correctCollateralValue,\n        1_000_000,\n        \"collateral overvalued by 10^6 - user can borrow 1M x more than they should\"\n    );\n}\n```\n\nRun with: `forge test --match-path \"test/c4/C4Submission.t.sol\" -vvv`\n\nResult: `[PASS] test_submissionValidity() (gas: 280578)` - all 9 assertions pass.\n\nThe test proves:\n1. 100 USDM deposit produces 100e12 raw shares (12-decimal internal precision)\n2. `decimals()` returns 6 while ERC-4626 compliant value should be 12\n3. An integrator using `decimals()=6` interprets 100e12 as 100,000,000 tokens instead of 100\n4. The overestimation factor is exactly 10^6\n5. `convertToAssets()` returns the correct value (internal share math is fine, only `decimals()` is wrong)\n6. A lending protocol scenario shows collateral overvalued by 10^6",
